1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,055
Watch almost any big rocket lift off,

2
00:00:03,055 --> 00:00:06,110
and sheets of white ice come tumbling

3
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:07,349
down its sides.

4
00:00:07,529 --> 00:00:09,702
It looks like the rocket is shaking

5
00:00:09,702 --> 00:00:10,882
off a coat of snow,

6
00:00:10,882 --> 00:00:13,055
on a hot summer morning in Florida.

7
00:00:13,235 --> 00:00:14,512
It is not snow.

8
00:00:14,692 --> 00:00:16,667
It is ice that formed on the

9
00:00:16,667 --> 00:00:19,066
outside of the rocket in the hours

10
00:00:19,066 --> 00:00:21,253
before launch, and it is one of

11
00:00:21,253 --> 00:00:23,934
the strangest problems in spaceflight.

12
00:00:24,114 --> 00:00:27,269
Today, ten parts of a rocket launch,

13
00:00:27,269 --> 00:00:29,373
and why each one exists.

14
00:00:29,722 --> 00:00:32,667
A rocket is mostly a flying tank

15
00:00:32,667 --> 00:00:34,692
of super cold liquids.

16
00:00:34,872 --> 00:00:36,906
Those liquids are what burn,

17
00:00:36,906 --> 00:00:39,304
but they are also what freeze the

18
00:00:39,304 --> 00:00:40,758
air around the tank.

19
00:00:40,938 --> 00:00:42,325
Over the decades,

20
00:00:42,325 --> 00:00:44,690
engineers have invented foam,

21
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:47,545
heaters, vents and a huge amount of

22
00:00:47,545 --> 00:00:49,503
water to deal with that,

23
00:00:49,503 --> 00:00:52,439
and some of those fixes were learned

24
00:00:52,439 --> 00:00:53,500
the hard way.

25
00:00:53,849 --> 00:00:56,980
As always, every fact here comes from

26
00:00:56,980 --> 00:00:58,250
a named source,

27
00:00:58,250 --> 00:01:03,327
NASA documents, accident reports and spaceflight references,

28
00:01:03,327 --> 00:01:05,866
all listed in the description.

29
00:01:06,046 --> 00:01:08,496
Let's start with the ice itself.

30
00:01:08,845 --> 00:01:11,097
Why does a rocket get covered in

31
00:01:11,097 --> 00:01:12,363
ice on a warm day?

32
00:01:12,543 --> 00:01:15,214
Because of what is inside.

33
00:01:15,394 --> 00:01:18,178
Liquid oxygen, the part of the fuel

34
00:01:18,178 --> 00:01:19,610
that lets it burn,

35
00:01:19,610 --> 00:01:22,077
has to be colder than minus one

36
00:01:22,077 --> 00:01:26,214
hundred eighty-three degrees Celsius to stay liquid.

37
00:01:26,394 --> 00:01:28,682
Liquid hydrogen is even colder,

38
00:01:28,682 --> 00:01:31,561
at about minus two hundred fifty-three.

39
00:01:31,741 --> 00:01:34,210
The metal skin of the tank gets

40
00:01:34,210 --> 00:01:35,245
just as cold,

41
00:01:35,245 --> 00:01:37,794
and water vapor in the air turns

42
00:01:37,794 --> 00:01:40,662
into solid ice the moment it touches

43
00:01:40,662 --> 00:01:40,901
it.

44
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:43,880
This is the same thing that happens

45
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:45,757
on a cold glass of water,

46
00:01:45,757 --> 00:01:47,485
only much more extreme.

47
00:01:47,665 --> 00:01:50,261
The air near Florida's coast is very

48
00:01:50,261 --> 00:01:52,208
humid, so there is a lot of

49
00:01:52,208 --> 00:01:53,795
water vapor to freeze.

50
00:01:53,975 --> 00:01:56,608
The ice grows thicker the longer the

51
00:01:56,608 --> 00:01:58,802
rocket sits on the pad full of

52
00:01:58,802 --> 00:01:59,606
propellant.

53
00:01:59,786 --> 00:02:01,664
When the engines light up,

54
00:02:01,664 --> 00:02:04,192
the vibration and the wind shake it

55
00:02:04,192 --> 00:02:06,647
loose, and it falls off in plates.

56
00:02:06,997 --> 00:02:08,877
Rockets that use kerosene,

57
00:02:08,877 --> 00:02:10,394
like the Falcon nine,

58
00:02:10,394 --> 00:02:12,779
have the same problem on the part

59
00:02:12,779 --> 00:02:15,164
of the tank that holds the liquid

60
00:02:15,164 --> 00:02:15,670
oxygen.

61
00:02:15,850 --> 00:02:19,108
SpaceX even chills its kerosene,

62
00:02:19,108 --> 00:02:22,876
to about minus seven degrees Celsius,

63
00:02:22,876 --> 00:02:24,709
to make it denser.

64
00:02:24,889 --> 00:02:27,246
The ice that you see falling off

65
00:02:27,246 --> 00:02:30,046
is nearly always from the oxygen tank,

66
00:02:30,046 --> 00:02:32,331
the coldest part of the rocket.

67
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:35,478
Why did the giant Saturn V rocket

68
00:02:35,478 --> 00:02:37,174
drop ice at liftoff?

69
00:02:37,354 --> 00:02:40,426
Because its liquid oxygen tanks were not

70
00:02:40,426 --> 00:02:42,346
insulated on the outside.

71
00:02:42,526 --> 00:02:44,741
The cold of the tank went straight

72
00:02:44,741 --> 00:02:47,021
through the metal wall and iced the

73
00:02:47,021 --> 00:02:49,301
surface, and chunks fell off as the

74
00:02:49,301 --> 00:02:50,734
rocket began to climb.

75
00:02:50,914 --> 00:02:53,974
Ice fragments falling away from the Saturn

76
00:02:53,974 --> 00:02:55,941
V at liftoff are one of the

77
00:02:55,941 --> 00:02:58,855
best known images of the Apollo program.

78
00:02:59,205 --> 00:03:02,237
The Saturn V stood about one hundred

79
00:03:02,237 --> 00:03:03,584
ten meters tall,

80
00:03:03,584 --> 00:03:07,288
with its first stage carrying enormous tanks

81
00:03:07,288 --> 00:03:09,225
of oxygen and kerosene.

82
00:03:09,405 --> 00:03:10,304
At the time,

83
00:03:10,304 --> 00:03:12,852
the falling ice was simply a known

84
00:03:12,852 --> 00:03:14,800
part of a Saturn V launch,

85
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:17,873
because nothing fragile was riding on the

86
00:03:17,873 --> 00:03:19,297
side of the rocket.

87
00:03:19,646 --> 00:03:23,066
That changed when rockets and spacecraft started

88
00:03:23,066 --> 00:03:25,916
to carry delicate things on their sides.

89
00:03:26,096 --> 00:03:28,036
A piece of ice that hits a

90
00:03:28,036 --> 00:03:30,275
solid tank is only a nuisance.

91
00:03:30,455 --> 00:03:31,821
A piece of ice,

92
00:03:31,821 --> 00:03:35,462
or foam, that hits something fragile can

93
00:03:35,462 --> 00:03:36,736
be a disaster.

94
00:03:36,916 --> 00:03:39,488
And that is exactly what happened with

95
00:03:39,488 --> 00:03:41,653
the next American rocket system.

96
00:03:42,003 --> 00:03:44,171
Why was the big tank under the

97
00:03:44,171 --> 00:03:46,845
Space Shuttle covered in orange foam?

98
00:03:47,025 --> 00:03:49,255
To keep the cold in and the

99
00:03:49,255 --> 00:03:49,916
ice out.

100
00:03:50,096 --> 00:03:53,467
The external tank held liquid hydrogen and

101
00:03:53,467 --> 00:03:56,677
liquid oxygen, and the shuttle sat right

102
00:03:56,677 --> 00:03:57,480
beside it.

103
00:03:57,660 --> 00:04:00,596
Ice breaking off could hit the orbiter,

104
00:04:00,596 --> 00:04:04,135
so engineers sprayed the tank with polyurethane

105
00:04:04,135 --> 00:04:06,845
foam, at least about one inch thick,

106
00:04:06,845 --> 00:04:09,480
which stops the outside of the tank

107
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:12,417
from getting cold enough to build thick

108
00:04:12,417 --> 00:04:12,718
ice.

109
00:04:13,067 --> 00:04:14,768
The foam did its job,

110
00:04:14,768 --> 00:04:16,225
but not perfectly.

111
00:04:16,405 --> 00:04:19,377
Moisture could get into small cracks and

112
00:04:19,377 --> 00:04:22,496
punctures, and then freeze and make pieces

113
00:04:22,496 --> 00:04:23,685
of foam pop off.

114
00:04:23,865 --> 00:04:26,429
The foam was thicker around the parts

115
00:04:26,429 --> 00:04:27,469
that stuck out,

116
00:04:27,469 --> 00:04:29,895
and the places where it was applied

117
00:04:29,895 --> 00:04:32,390
by hand, like around the supports of

118
00:04:32,390 --> 00:04:34,677
the tank, were the ones that shed

119
00:04:34,677 --> 00:04:35,301
the most.

120
00:04:35,481 --> 00:04:38,198
The orange color is the natural color

121
00:04:38,198 --> 00:04:39,080
of the foam.

122
00:04:39,429 --> 00:04:42,259
So the shuttle program lived with a

123
00:04:42,259 --> 00:04:43,795
strange compromise.

124
00:04:43,975 --> 00:04:46,565
Without foam, the tank would drop big

125
00:04:46,565 --> 00:04:48,735
sheets of ice onto the orbiter.

126
00:04:48,915 --> 00:04:51,674
With foam, the tank could drop foam

127
00:04:51,674 --> 00:04:52,305
instead.

128
00:04:52,485 --> 00:04:54,996
Both fall during the first minute of

129
00:04:54,996 --> 00:04:56,531
flight, and for years,

130
00:04:56,531 --> 00:04:59,112
engineers watched every launch video,

131
00:04:59,112 --> 00:05:00,716
looking for the pieces.

132
00:05:01,066 --> 00:05:03,356
Why did a piece of foam destroy

133
00:05:03,356 --> 00:05:04,538
a space shuttle?

134
00:05:04,718 --> 00:05:06,834
On the first of February,

135
00:05:06,834 --> 00:05:08,441
two thousand three,

136
00:05:08,441 --> 00:05:12,504
Columbia broke apart while returning from orbit,

137
00:05:12,504 --> 00:05:14,958
killing all seven astronauts.

138
00:05:15,138 --> 00:05:18,198
The investigation traced the damage to a

139
00:05:18,198 --> 00:05:20,569
piece of foam that fell off the

140
00:05:20,569 --> 00:05:23,323
tank during launch and hit the wing.

141
00:05:23,503 --> 00:05:26,187
Later tests fired a piece of foam

142
00:05:26,187 --> 00:05:29,441
weighing about one and two thirds pounds

143
00:05:29,441 --> 00:05:32,369
at five hundred thirty miles an hour

144
00:05:32,369 --> 00:05:33,833
into a wing panel,

145
00:05:33,833 --> 00:05:36,517
and it blew a hole sixteen inches

146
00:05:36,517 --> 00:05:36,924
wide.

147
00:05:37,273 --> 00:05:39,613
A hole in the wing's heat shield

148
00:05:39,613 --> 00:05:41,952
let the hot gases of reentry in.

149
00:05:42,132 --> 00:05:44,123
A lot of foam had come off

150
00:05:44,123 --> 00:05:48,106
earlier shuttle launches without causing a disaster,

151
00:05:48,106 --> 00:05:50,863
which made people think it was safe.

152
00:05:51,043 --> 00:05:54,967
The investigation called that a cultural problem.

153
00:05:55,147 --> 00:05:57,563
A danger that does not cause a

154
00:05:57,563 --> 00:06:00,381
crash the first few times starts to

155
00:06:00,381 --> 00:06:01,347
feel normal.

156
00:06:01,696 --> 00:06:03,162
After the accident,

157
00:06:03,162 --> 00:06:05,552
NASA spent about one and a half

158
00:06:05,552 --> 00:06:08,328
billion dollars on fixes to the tank

159
00:06:08,328 --> 00:06:10,102
and on safety upgrades.

160
00:06:10,282 --> 00:06:11,282
A foam ramp,

161
00:06:11,282 --> 00:06:13,197
thirty-seven feet long,

162
00:06:13,197 --> 00:06:16,029
was removed from the tank after it

163
00:06:16,029 --> 00:06:18,862
was blamed for a piece that nearly

164
00:06:18,862 --> 00:06:22,277
hit Discovery's wing in July two thousand

165
00:06:22,277 --> 00:06:22,693
five.

166
00:06:22,873 --> 00:06:26,371
Foam and ice stopped being small details,

167
00:06:26,371 --> 00:06:29,358
and became a safety issue for every

168
00:06:29,358 --> 00:06:29,955
launch.

169
00:06:30,305 --> 00:06:32,478
Why would a rocket full of super

170
00:06:32,478 --> 00:06:35,193
cold liquid have heaters on its outside?

171
00:06:35,373 --> 00:06:37,688
To stop ice from forming in the

172
00:06:37,688 --> 00:06:38,659
worst places.

173
00:06:38,839 --> 00:06:41,276
On the shuttle's external tank,

174
00:06:41,276 --> 00:06:44,814
the long feedline that carried liquid oxygen,

175
00:06:44,814 --> 00:06:48,274
about seventy feet long and seventeen inches

176
00:06:48,274 --> 00:06:51,733
wide, had flexible joints with strip heaters

177
00:06:51,733 --> 00:06:54,328
of about half an inch wide around

178
00:06:54,328 --> 00:06:54,721
them.

179
00:06:54,901 --> 00:06:56,880
The heat kept ice from building up

180
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:58,742
where it could break off and hit

181
00:06:58,742 --> 00:06:59,441
the orbiter.

182
00:06:59,790 --> 00:07:03,138
After Columbia, NASA also added heaters at

183
00:07:03,138 --> 00:07:04,971
key points of the tank,

184
00:07:04,971 --> 00:07:07,681
to keep ice from forming under the

185
00:07:07,681 --> 00:07:08,080
foam.

186
00:07:08,260 --> 00:07:09,755
It is an odd sight,

187
00:07:09,755 --> 00:07:12,746
a machine carrying hundreds of tons of

188
00:07:12,746 --> 00:07:16,051
liquid hydrogen at minus two hundred fifty

189
00:07:16,051 --> 00:07:19,750
degrees, with small electric heaters stuck onto

190
00:07:19,750 --> 00:07:19,986
it.

191
00:07:20,166 --> 00:07:22,220
But where ice is dangerous,

192
00:07:22,220 --> 00:07:24,578
a little heat is cheaper than a

193
00:07:24,578 --> 00:07:25,263
big risk.

194
00:07:25,612 --> 00:07:28,050
The vent system of the shuttle used

195
00:07:28,050 --> 00:07:29,026
the same idea.

196
00:07:29,206 --> 00:07:32,667
Warm, dry nitrogen gas was pumped into

197
00:07:32,667 --> 00:07:33,942
the vent hood,

198
00:07:33,942 --> 00:07:37,312
so the escaping oxygen did not freeze

199
00:07:37,312 --> 00:07:39,225
water vapor into ice.

200
00:07:39,405 --> 00:07:41,727
No water, no ice.

201
00:07:42,077 --> 00:07:44,327
Why did the shuttle tank wear a

202
00:07:44,327 --> 00:07:46,431
big cap on top before launch?

203
00:07:46,611 --> 00:07:48,886
It was called the beanie cap,

204
00:07:48,886 --> 00:07:51,317
a vent hood thirteen feet wide,

205
00:07:51,317 --> 00:07:53,670
on an arm of the launch tower.

206
00:07:53,850 --> 00:07:56,466
Liquid oxygen in the tank is always

207
00:07:56,466 --> 00:07:58,036
boiling off into gas,

208
00:07:58,036 --> 00:08:00,578
and the hood collected the gas and

209
00:08:00,578 --> 00:08:03,045
carried it away from the shuttle.

210
00:08:03,225 --> 00:08:05,818
It was warmed with nitrogen gas so

211
00:08:05,818 --> 00:08:08,715
the vented oxygen did not freeze water

212
00:08:08,715 --> 00:08:11,003
vapor into ice near the top of

213
00:08:11,003 --> 00:08:11,689
the tank.

214
00:08:12,038 --> 00:08:14,391
The cap lifted off the tank about

215
00:08:14,391 --> 00:08:17,030
two and a half minutes before launch,

216
00:08:17,030 --> 00:08:20,096
in a process that took twenty-five seconds.

217
00:08:20,276 --> 00:08:22,556
The arm then swung back to the

218
00:08:22,556 --> 00:08:26,204
tower about one minute forty-five seconds before

219
00:08:26,204 --> 00:08:26,812
liftoff.

220
00:08:26,992 --> 00:08:29,269
And it was not latched during the

221
00:08:29,269 --> 00:08:31,614
countdown, so if there was a hold,

222
00:08:31,614 --> 00:08:34,097
the hood could come back down again.

223
00:08:34,447 --> 00:08:36,066
It is a small detail,

224
00:08:36,066 --> 00:08:38,609
but it shows how the countdown is

225
00:08:38,609 --> 00:08:39,765
a choreography.

226
00:08:39,945 --> 00:08:42,667
Every arm and every hose has its

227
00:08:42,667 --> 00:08:44,368
exact time to leave.

228
00:08:44,548 --> 00:08:46,959
If the launch team sees a problem

229
00:08:46,959 --> 00:08:48,494
before the cap lifts,

230
00:08:48,494 --> 00:08:50,905
the whole thing can stop and come

231
00:08:50,905 --> 00:08:51,270
back.

232
00:08:51,450 --> 00:08:52,423
On a rocket,

233
00:08:52,423 --> 00:08:55,340
a few seconds can matter enormously.

234
00:08:55,689 --> 00:08:58,239
Why do rockets hiss out big white

235
00:08:58,239 --> 00:08:59,939
clouds before liftoff?

236
00:09:00,119 --> 00:09:01,384
It is boil-off.

237
00:09:01,564 --> 00:09:04,025
The liquid oxygen in the tank warms

238
00:09:04,025 --> 00:09:05,783
up a little all the time,

239
00:09:05,783 --> 00:09:07,752
and some of it turns to gas.

240
00:09:07,932 --> 00:09:09,902
If the gas stayed inside,

241
00:09:09,902 --> 00:09:12,108
the pressure would build up,

242
00:09:12,108 --> 00:09:13,842
so valves vent it out.

243
00:09:14,022 --> 00:09:16,090
The vapor is so cold that it

244
00:09:16,090 --> 00:09:18,528
freezes the water in the air into

245
00:09:18,528 --> 00:09:19,563
a white cloud,

246
00:09:19,563 --> 00:09:21,853
just like your breath on a cold

247
00:09:21,853 --> 00:09:22,961
winter morning.

248
00:09:23,311 --> 00:09:25,738
A Falcon nine does the same,

249
00:09:25,738 --> 00:09:28,771
venting liquid oxygen on the pad as

250
00:09:28,771 --> 00:09:30,591
the propellant warms.

251
00:09:30,771 --> 00:09:33,060
The white cloud is a sign that

252
00:09:33,060 --> 00:09:35,348
the rocket is fueled and cold,

253
00:09:35,348 --> 00:09:37,866
and that its tanks are doing what

254
00:09:37,866 --> 00:09:38,782
they should.

255
00:09:39,131 --> 00:09:41,732
There is another white cloud at liftoff,

256
00:09:41,732 --> 00:09:44,333
and it has a completely different cause.

257
00:09:44,513 --> 00:09:46,312
When the engines fire,

258
00:09:46,312 --> 00:09:48,929
huge amounts of water on the pad

259
00:09:48,929 --> 00:09:50,074
turn to steam.

260
00:09:50,254 --> 00:09:52,193
That is the next part on our

261
00:09:52,193 --> 00:09:54,617
list, and it might surprise you how

262
00:09:54,617 --> 00:09:56,210
much water is involved.

263
00:09:56,559 --> 00:09:58,926
Why does a launch pad flood with

264
00:09:58,926 --> 00:10:01,145
water as the rocket lifts off?

265
00:10:01,325 --> 00:10:02,254
The noise.

266
00:10:02,434 --> 00:10:05,750
A rocket engine's exhaust can reach around

267
00:10:05,750 --> 00:10:08,434
two hundred decibels near liftoff,

268
00:10:08,434 --> 00:10:11,197
so loud that the sound waves bounce

269
00:10:11,197 --> 00:10:13,566
off the pad and can damage the

270
00:10:13,566 --> 00:10:14,671
rocket itself.

271
00:10:14,851 --> 00:10:17,919
Water poured into the exhaust absorbs a

272
00:10:17,919 --> 00:10:20,122
lot of that acoustic energy.

273
00:10:20,302 --> 00:10:22,130
At the Space Shuttle pad,

274
00:10:22,130 --> 00:10:24,617
it brought the sound down to about

275
00:10:24,617 --> 00:10:26,885
one hundred forty-two decibels.

276
00:10:27,234 --> 00:10:30,210
The shuttle pad's water tower held about

277
00:10:30,210 --> 00:10:32,517
three hundred thousand gallons.

278
00:10:32,697 --> 00:10:35,732
The water started flowing about six and

279
00:10:35,732 --> 00:10:38,689
a half seconds before the main engines

280
00:10:38,689 --> 00:10:42,113
lit, and drained in about forty-one seconds.

281
00:10:42,293 --> 00:10:44,225
For the huge SLS rocket,

282
00:10:44,225 --> 00:10:47,365
the system was upgraded to four hundred

283
00:10:47,365 --> 00:10:50,182
thousand gallons, at a peak flow of

284
00:10:50,182 --> 00:10:53,322
one point one million gallons a minute.

285
00:10:53,502 --> 00:10:56,100
Much of that water boils into the

286
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:58,541
white clouds you see at launch.

287
00:10:58,890 --> 00:11:01,861
So water protects the rocket from its

288
00:11:01,861 --> 00:11:02,664
own sound.

289
00:11:02,844 --> 00:11:05,694
Without it, the sound reflecting off the

290
00:11:05,694 --> 00:11:08,259
pad could damage the rocket and what

291
00:11:08,259 --> 00:11:09,043
it carries.

292
00:11:09,223 --> 00:11:11,661
A rocket does not just fight gravity.

293
00:11:11,841 --> 00:11:13,757
It fights its own noise.

294
00:11:14,107 --> 00:11:16,181
Why is there a giant trench under

295
00:11:16,181 --> 00:11:16,998
a launch pad?

296
00:11:17,178 --> 00:11:19,699
Because the exhaust of a rocket has

297
00:11:19,699 --> 00:11:21,068
nowhere good to go.

298
00:11:21,248 --> 00:11:22,817
If it hit flat ground,

299
00:11:22,817 --> 00:11:25,455
it would bounce straight back up into

300
00:11:25,455 --> 00:11:26,240
the rocket.

301
00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:29,419
The trench and the flame deflector guide

302
00:11:29,419 --> 00:11:31,668
the exhaust away from the pad.

303
00:11:31,848 --> 00:11:34,538
They also redirect the sound energy,

304
00:11:34,538 --> 00:11:37,676
which makes the water system's job easier.

305
00:11:38,025 --> 00:11:40,742
The deflector is a huge curved or

306
00:11:40,742 --> 00:11:44,365
wedge-shaped surface, built to take the full

307
00:11:44,365 --> 00:11:46,094
blast of the engines.

308
00:11:46,274 --> 00:11:48,956
The concrete and steel around it are

309
00:11:48,956 --> 00:11:52,010
covered in special heat-resistant layers.

310
00:11:52,190 --> 00:11:54,721
Even so, a pad can need repairs

311
00:11:54,721 --> 00:11:55,946
after a launch,

312
00:11:55,946 --> 00:11:58,642
because the exhaust is one of the

313
00:11:58,642 --> 00:12:01,663
harshest things a structure can face.

314
00:12:02,013 --> 00:12:04,551
Not every launch site looks the same,

315
00:12:04,551 --> 00:12:06,608
but all of them solve the same

316
00:12:06,608 --> 00:12:07,157
problem.

317
00:12:07,337 --> 00:12:10,145
Whatever comes out of the engines needs

318
00:12:10,145 --> 00:12:11,945
a safe and quick way out,

319
00:12:11,945 --> 00:12:14,393
or the rocket will have to survive

320
00:12:14,393 --> 00:12:15,545
its own exhaust.

321
00:12:15,894 --> 00:12:17,820
Why do the arms on a launch

322
00:12:17,820 --> 00:12:20,387
tower pull away just before liftoff?

323
00:12:20,567 --> 00:12:22,506
They carry the propellant,

324
00:12:22,506 --> 00:12:25,117
power and data lines to the rocket,

325
00:12:25,117 --> 00:12:27,876
called umbilicals, like the cord of a

326
00:12:27,876 --> 00:12:28,845
newborn baby.

327
00:12:29,025 --> 00:12:30,759
Until the last moments,

328
00:12:30,759 --> 00:12:33,321
the rocket is fed from the ground.

329
00:12:33,501 --> 00:12:35,971
Then each line has to disconnect,

330
00:12:35,971 --> 00:12:37,394
in the right order,

331
00:12:37,394 --> 00:12:39,490
and the arm has to swing out

332
00:12:39,490 --> 00:12:41,361
of the way of the rocket.

333
00:12:41,711 --> 00:12:43,404
On the Space Shuttle,

334
00:12:43,404 --> 00:12:46,225
the oxygen vent arm with the beanie

335
00:12:46,225 --> 00:12:50,174
cap retracted about one minute forty-five seconds

336
00:12:50,174 --> 00:12:51,382
before liftoff.

337
00:12:51,562 --> 00:12:54,281
But it stayed unlatched until the very

338
00:12:54,281 --> 00:12:56,428
end, so it could return if the

339
00:12:56,428 --> 00:12:57,716
countdown stopped.

340
00:12:57,896 --> 00:13:00,765
That way, a delay meant going back

341
00:13:00,765 --> 00:13:03,550
one step, not starting from zero.

342
00:13:03,730 --> 00:13:04,624
On a rocket,

343
00:13:04,624 --> 00:13:07,010
every system has a plan for when

344
00:13:07,010 --> 00:13:08,873
things do not go to plan.

345
00:13:09,223 --> 00:13:10,891
And when it all works,

346
00:13:10,891 --> 00:13:13,088
the last thing you see is the

347
00:13:13,088 --> 00:13:13,391
ice.

348
00:13:13,571 --> 00:13:16,352
Once the arms have retreated and the

349
00:13:16,352 --> 00:13:17,664
engines have lit,

350
00:13:17,664 --> 00:13:20,599
the rocket shrugs off the white plates

351
00:13:20,599 --> 00:13:22,221
of frost, and climbs.

352
00:13:22,401 --> 00:13:24,545
What fell off was the cold,

353
00:13:24,545 --> 00:13:27,405
the part the rocket no longer needs.

354
00:13:27,754 --> 00:13:29,159
So here are the ten.

355
00:13:29,339 --> 00:13:31,122
Ice from the cold tanks,

356
00:13:31,122 --> 00:13:33,054
and a Saturn V that let it

357
00:13:33,054 --> 00:13:33,426
fall.

358
00:13:33,606 --> 00:13:36,646
Orange foam that stopped the ice and

359
00:13:36,646 --> 00:13:38,927
became a danger of its own,

360
00:13:38,927 --> 00:13:42,305
a piece of foam that destroyed Columbia,

361
00:13:42,305 --> 00:13:45,007
heaters on the frozen feedlines,

362
00:13:45,007 --> 00:13:47,457
and a cap that vented oxygen.

363
00:13:47,637 --> 00:13:49,562
White clouds of boil-off,

364
00:13:49,562 --> 00:13:52,335
a water tower that fights the noise,

365
00:13:52,335 --> 00:13:54,106
a trench for the flame,

366
00:13:54,106 --> 00:13:56,186
and arms that let go at the

367
00:13:56,186 --> 00:13:57,110
last moment.

368
00:13:57,460 --> 00:13:59,856
Next time you see ice falling off

369
00:13:59,856 --> 00:14:02,107
a rocket, you will know what it

370
00:14:02,107 --> 00:14:02,325
is.

371
00:14:02,505 --> 00:14:04,483
A rocket at the pad is a

372
00:14:04,483 --> 00:14:06,873
cold machine in a warm world,

373
00:14:06,873 --> 00:14:09,840
and everything around it is there to

374
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:10,829
manage that.

375
00:14:11,009 --> 00:14:14,034
If you want more machines explained part

376
00:14:14,034 --> 00:14:16,907
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

377
00:14:17,087 --> 00:14:19,095
And tell us in the comments which

378
00:14:19,095 --> 00:14:21,102
machine you want us to take apart

